hi this is Mario with Mario's math  tutoring coming to another math viewer  to help you boost your score in your  math class improve your understanding  and hopefully make learning math a lot  less stressful so we're going to talk  about in this video is how to find the  tangent line equation to a parabola  using the distance formula so you're  probably doing a problem like this  similar in your class and so I just want  to show you how this works  so you have a parabola here okay this  u-shaped graph and you want to find the  equation of the tangent line to the  parabola at a given point so that's the  point right there and what you want to  notice or pay attention to is that the  distance from the focus of the parabola  to the point where the tangent line  touches the parabola that distance is  going to be the same distance as from  the focus to the y-intercept  that the tangent line you know makes  with the y-axis so that's what we're  going to be making use of is that this  distance and this distance are the same  so I'll show you what I mean in this  example so here what we want to do is we  want to find the equation of the tangent  line okay to this parabola x squared  equals negative 2y through the point  negative 4 negative 8 so I've drawn a  little sketch here for us just so we can  see it a little bit better and so what  we're going to do first thing is we're  going to find the focus so if you notice  this is in the form x squared equals 4p  Y and if you need to reference how to  graph parabolas you can I'll have a link  for the video for that but basically you  can see that for p equals negative 2 so  that means that p equals negative 1/2  right so if we go down here this is  going to be negative 1/2 right there  okay that's our focus so it focus is at  0 comma negative 1/2 and we want to find  the distance from that point to the  point where the tangent line just barely  touches the parabola okay and this point  over here is negative 4 negative 8 so  we're going to be doing is we're gonna  be doing the distance formula so let's  go ahead and do that so we have the  distance equals the square root of 0  minus negative 4 squared plus negative  1/2 minus negative 8 squared add those  together and take the square root so if  we simplify this we get  16-plus  this comes out to seven-and-a-half which  is 15 halves which if we square that we  get to 225 force and if we get common  denominators here this is 64 force plus  225 force is 289 force and if we take  the square root of that we're going to  get 17 over 2 which is 8 and 1/2 okay so  what that means that this distance here  is 8 and a half the distance from the  focus to the y-intercept okay that the  tangent line crosses the y axis over  here this distance is also going to be 8  and a half and because the focus is over  here 1/2 unit below the x-axis that's  going to mean that this y-intercept here  is going to be at 0 comma 8 okay now the  only thing we need to figure out now is  the slope of the tangent line we already  have the y-intercept and we'll be able  to write our equation so to find the  slope we use the slope formula okay  which is y2 minus y1 over x2 minus x1  and so we're just going to take 8 minus  negative 8 which is 16 over 0 minus  negative 4 which is 4 so you can see  we're getting a slope of 4 okay so with  me so far  we know the slope we know the  y-intercept so the equation of our  tangent line to the parabola at this  point negative 4 negative 8 is going to  be y equals 4x plus 8 and that's it so  again just a quick recap if you want to  find the tangent line to a parabola at a  given point just realize that the  distance from that point to the focus of  the parabola and the distance from the  focus to the y intercept that the  tangent line you know makes when it  crosses the y axis those two distances  are equal and you can use that to find  the equation of the tangent line so  hopefully you found this video helpful  go ahead and subscribe t